.TH JOIN 1
.SH NAME
join \- relational join of two sorted files, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B join
[
.BI -a\  n
]
[
.BI -e\  string
]
[
.BI -o\  list
]
[
.BI -t\  char
]
[
.BI -1\  field
]
[
.BI -2\  field
]
[
.BI -j\  field
]
[
.BI -j1\  field
]
[
.BI -j2\  field
]
.I file1 file2
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Join
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR join (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/join.c ,
368 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike reverse-
engineered from a host tool \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Join
reads
.I file1
and
.IR file2 ,
each already sorted on its join field (this program does not sort them
itself), and writes one output line for every pair of lines \(em one
from each file \(em whose join fields are equal: the join field's
value, then
.IR file1 's
remaining fields, then
.IR file2 's
remaining fields. If either
.I file1
or
.I file2
is
.BR - ,
standard input is read for that one \(em but not both:
.I join
picks whichever file is randomly seekable to drive its match loop
(needed to replay a run of duplicate keys on the other side without
buffering it), and errors if neither is, which is exactly what
.B join - -
does.
.PP
Lines with a repeated key on either side produce every combination, not
just the first: a key appearing twice in
.I file1
and twice in
.I file2
produces four output lines.
.PP
The default field separator is a run of one or more spaces and/or tabs
(leading whitespace before the first field is skipped entirely); the
default
.I output
field separator is always a single space unless
.B -t
was given, in which case it is whatever string was attached to
.BR -t 's
argument (see below \(em usually just the one separator character, but
not always).
.SS Options
.TP
.BI -a\  n
In addition to the default matched output, also emit a line for every
unpairable line in file
.I n
(1 or 2).
.I N
may be attached
.RB ( -a1 )
or a separate word
.RB ( "-a 1" ).
.TP
.BI -e\  string
Replace an empty (or, for
.BR -o ,
out-of-range) output field with
.IR string .
.TP
.BI -o\ list
Choose exactly which fields appear, and in what order, instead of the
default format.
.I List
is either a single comma-separated argument (e.g.
.BR 0,1.2,2.2 ,
attachable directly as
.BR -o0,1.2,2.2 )
or, historically, one field spec per following word (e.g.
.BR "-o 0 1.2 2.2" ) \(em
both forms are real and both are ported; a later
.B -o
entirely replaces an earlier one rather than concatenating with it
(checked against the real binary, not assumed \(em see BUGS). Each
element is either
.B 0
(the join field's value) or
.IB filenum . field
(1-based field
.I field
of file
.IR filenum ).
.TP
.BI -t\  char
Use
.I char
as the field separator for splitting, in place of the whitespace-run
default; every occurrence of
.I char
is a separator (no collapsing). Only the first character of
.BR -t 's
argument is used for splitting \(em but the whole argument becomes the
output separator, so an accidentally multi-character
.B -t
argument still splits on just its first character while printing all
of it between output fields (a real, easy-to-miss detail of the
original, carried over rather than tidied up).
.TP
.BI -1\  field\  ", " -2\  field
Join on
.IR field 's-th
field (1-based) of
.I file1
/
.I file2
instead of the first.
.TP
.BI -j\  field\  ", " -j1\  field\  ", " -j2\  field
Historic synonyms for
.BR -1 / -2 :
plain
.B -j
sets both fields at once,
.B -j1
is the same as
.BR -1 ,
.B -j2
is the same as
.BR -2 .
Unlike
.BR -1 / -2 ,
.BR -j 's
value is always a separate following word, never attached.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/join.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/join.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR jointest (1)
.SH BUGS
An unrecognised single-dash option is silently ignored, not an error \(em
the original's option switch has no default case, and this is translated
faithfully rather than "improved".
.PP
A line with 100 or more fields is truncated at the 100th (matching the
original's fixed
.B NFLD
limit); if this happens anywhere in either input,
.I join
still produces whatever output the truncated data leads to, and only
reports the failure at the very end, after that output has already been
emitted \(em also a faithful translation of the original's own ordering,
not a defect introduced here.
.PP
Field comparison is plain code-point-by-code-point string comparison
(Limbo strings are already rune-indexed, matching the original's own
UTF-32 Rune-based comparison exactly, with no conversion step needed),
not locale-aware collation.
.PP
Three real bugs in the original are preserved rather than fixed, and are
documented in detail in
.BR join.b 's
header comment (all three checked against a real Plan 9 join binary,
not just read off the source): a multi-digit
.B -o
field number in the comma-list form
.RB ( "-o 1.10,2.2" )
silently inserts a spurious extra join-key output column, from a scan-
pointer misalignment in the original's
.BR oparse (); an
.B -o
spec referencing a nonexistent field 0
.RB ( "-o 1.0" )
is undefined behaviour in the original (confirmed: segfaults the real
binary) \(em this translation substitutes the
.B -e
replacement string instead of a crash, since there is no well-defined
value to reproduce; and a join field configured past some line's actual
field count reads memory-layout-dependent leftover data in the original
(also confirmed capable of segfaulting it), which this translation
replaces with "the last value actually written to that field index,
forever" \(em safe and well-defined, but not a faithful reproduction of
the original's own crash-prone behaviour, since there is nothing
well-defined there to be faithful to.
.PP
.B join - -
(both files from standard input, neither seekable) is documented as a
hard error by the original and by this translation's logic, but is not
exercised by
.IR jointest (1),
whose seam takes two real file paths directly rather than driving
.IR join 's
own
.BR init ,
standard input included \(em see
.IR jointest (1)'s
own BUGS.
